<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tarazona</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Turiaso, Turiasso</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tarazona is a town in the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a> in Aragon, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, near the Moncayo massif, known in Roman times as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turiaso">Turiaso</a>. It was an episcopal see from late antiquity, taken from the Muslims by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-i-of-aragon">Alfonso I of Aragon</a> in 1119. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corte">Cortes</a> of Tarazona of 1592 curtailed Aragon's traditional liberties after the revolt of Zaragoza.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tarazona.jpg" alt="Tarazona" width="320" height="204" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Diego Delso · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vista_de_Tarazona,_Espa%C3%B1a,_2015-01-02,_DD_07.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
